# THIS FILE PINS NO REGISTRY AND NO TOKEN, and the reasoning is the SDK repository's own (its
# `.npmrc` carries the long form; this is the short one, plus what is specific to the router).
#
# A committed `@yanlinglabs:registry=...` line would be GLOBAL, and there are two registries:
# pnpm's `pickRegistryForPackage` is `(scope && registries[scope]) ?? registries.default`, and
# `--registry` sets only `registries.default` -- so a scope pin here beats the CLI flag, in
# `pnpm publish` and `pnpm view` alike. `release.yml` gets its registry AND its credential together,
# per job, from `actions/setup-node` (`registry-url` + `scope`), which writes a throwaway userconfig
# and points `NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG` at it. That is the property a committed pin can never have.
#
# A `${NODE_AUTH_TOKEN}` placeholder is absent for the same reason: it authenticates nothing locally
# and makes every pnpm invocation print `WARN Issue while reading ".npmrc"`.
#
# ROUTER-SPECIFIC. This package's three `@yanlinglabs/*` devDependencies (`winter-agent-sdk`,
# `winter-conformance`, `winter-provider-conformance`) are registry pins, `^0.0.3`, resolved from npm
# -- the DEFAULT registry -- so no line here is needed for them, and none must be added: a scope line
# naming GitHub Packages would redirect THAT install too, and it carries no credential. `release.yml`
# orders `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` BEFORE `setup-node` writes its per-job scope pin for exactly
# this reason, and `test/gates/release-gates.test.ts` asserts the order.
#
# Phase 8a Task 0 landed the pin; R6 collapsed the temporary `link:` shape it replaced (R-7b-5) --
# no sibling checkout, no `tsconfig` `paths` -- see docs/architecture.md, "Consuming the Winter SDK",
# for the history.
